# .NET Quality and Performance Standards

- Prefer solutions that balance performance, readability, and long-term maintainability.
- Use async/await correctly for I/O-bound work and propagate `CancellationToken` where applicable.
- Keep nullable reference types enabled and handle nullability explicitly.
- Implement clear error handling: do not swallow exceptions; log useful context and rethrow or translate appropriately.
- Use structured logging with meaningful properties instead of ad-hoc string-only logs.
- Add or update tests (unit/integration as appropriate) for changed behavior and bug fixes.
- Prefer built-in analyzers and consistent formatting/naming conventions to keep code quality high.
- Optimize based on evidence (measure first), then improve hotspots without sacrificing clarity.
